If an asteroid were detected that would destroy life on Earth in 10 years, interest rates would likely fall sharply. Investors would rush to safety, driving up demand for low-risk assets like government bonds, which pushes their prices up and yields down. At the same time, uncertainty about the future would reduce business investment and consumer spending, weakening economic growth and further pressuring rates downward. Central banks might also cut rates to stimulate the economy, though the ultimate effect could be complicated by panic or collapse.

Conclusion: Interest rates would likely decline significantly due to flight to safety and weakened economic expectations. [end of text]


